# Model Context Protocol (MCP) Integration Guidelines

## Overview
The BrogueMCP project uses Model Context Protocol (MCP) to integrate AI-driven narrative generation and dungeon master features. This document outlines the implementation patterns and best practices.

## Server Architecture

### Simple MCP Server
The primary implementation is `dm-agent/server.js`, which provides:
- File system access to the codebase
- Template-based narrative generation
- Memory storage for procedural dungeon experiences

```javascript
// Example narrative generation endpoint
app.post('/mcp/tools/generateNarrative', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const { event_type, event_data, entity_id, style, tone } = req.body;
    // Generate narrative based on templates or AI
    let narrative = generateTemplateNarrative(event_type, eventData, style, tone);
    
    // Store in dungeon memories
    if (entity_id) {
      storeDungeonMemory(entity_id, narrative);
    }
    
    res.json(narrative);
  } catch (error) {
    // Provide fallback response
    res.json({ 
      text: "The dungeon falls silent...",
      tone: "mysterious",
      is_spoken: false,
      importance: 30
    });
  }
});
```

### Client Integration

The C client (`src/mcp/mcp_client.c`) provides a bridge between the game and the MCP server:

```c
/**
 * Request AI-generated content with fallback
 */
narrative_text_t *
mcp_generate_narrative(mcp_client_t *client, const char *event_type,
                     const char *event_data, const char *entity_id,
                     const char *style, const char *tone)
{
    // Try to get response from MCP server
    mcp_response_t *response = mcp_invoke_tool(client, "generateNarrative", json_params_str);
    
    // Process response or use fallback
    narrative_text_t *narrative = process_narrative_response(response);
    
    return narrative;
}
```

## Implementation Patterns

### Fallback Mechanism
Always implement fallback mechanisms for when the MCP server is unavailable:

```c
char *
get_ai_response(game_state_t *state, const char *prompt_type, const char *context)
{
    char *response = NULL;
    
    /* Try primary AI system */
    response = mcp_request(state, prompt_type, context);
    
    /* Fall back to template if AI unavailable */
    if (response == NULL) {
        response = get_template_response(prompt_type, context);
    }
    
    return response;
}
```

### Asynchronous Processing
Use asynchronous processing to avoid blocking the game:

```c
bool
request_narrative_async(const char *event_type, const char *event_data)
{
    return mcp_generate_narrative_async(
        mcp_client,
        event_type,
        event_data,
        player_uuid,
        "standard",
        NULL,
        narrative_callback,
        NULL
    );
}
```

### Memory Management
Store important dungeon and player memories for future reference:

```javascript
// Store dungeon memories
function storeDungeonMemory(dungeonId, narrative) {
  if (!dungeonMemories[dungeonId]) {
    dungeonMemories[dungeonId] = [];
  }
  
  dungeonMemories[dungeonId].push({
    timestamp: new Date(),
    narrative: narrative.text,
    importance: narrative.importance
  });
  
  // Sort by importance
  dungeonMemories[dungeonId].sort((a, b) => b.importance - a.importance);
}
```

## Testing

### Server Testing
Test the MCP server independently:

```bash
# Start the server
cd dm-agent
node server.js

# Test with curl
curl -X POST http://localhost:8100/mcp/tools/generateNarrative \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"event_type":"monster_death","event_data":{"monster_name":"Ancient Dragon"}}'
```

### Client Testing
Test the C client with the test program:

```bash
# Compile the test client
gcc -o test-dm test-dm.c src/mcp/mcp_client.c -lcurl -ljansson

# Run the test
./test-dm
```

## Dungeon Master UI Integration

The dungeon master UI is integrated through a simple interface:

```c
/**
 * Initialize DM data
 */
errr
dm_init(dm_state_t *state)
{
    /* Allocate DM data */
    state->data = mem_zalloc(sizeof(struct dm_data));
    
    /* Initialize MCP client */
    state->data->mcp_client = mcp_client_init(MCP_SERVER_URL, NULL);
    
    return 0;
}

/**
 * Render DM window
 */
void
render_dm_window(dm_state_t *state)
{
    /* Render DM interface */
    render_dm_controls(state);
    
    /* Render dialogue text */
    render_dm_dialogue(state);
    
    /* Render interaction options */
    render_dm_options(state);
}
```

## Deployment

### Server Startup
Use the provided scripts to start the server:

```batch
@echo off
echo Starting MCP server...
cd dm-agent
node server.js
```

### Game Integration
Start the game with the DM integration enabled:

```batch
@echo off
echo Starting MCP server in background...
start "MCP Server" cmd /c "cd dm-agent && node server.js"
echo Starting game...
brogue.exe -dm
```

## Procedural Content Integration

The MCP system provides valuable capabilities for enhancing procedural generation:

```c
/**
 * Get AI-enhanced description for a generated dungeon feature
 */
char *
get_feature_description(const char *feature_type, const char *context, int depth)
{
    json_t *params = json_object();
    json_object_set_new(params, "feature_type", json_string(feature_type));
    json_object_set_new(params, "context", json_string(context));
    json_object_set_new(params, "depth", json_integer(depth));
    
    char *params_str = json_dumps(params, 0);
    mcp_response_t *response = mcp_invoke_tool(mcp_client, "featureDescription", params_str);
    free(params_str);
    json_decref(params);
    
    if (response && response->success) {
        return strdup(response->result);
    }
    
    return get_default_description(feature_type);
}
```

This integration allows for rich, contextual descriptions of procedurally generated dungeon features that enhance the player experience.